# Experiment 15: electricity, in 1890, out of two crates of imported machinery.
#
# This is the design the whole experiment is an argument about. The Mining
# Valley has a river, timber, and neither a grid nor a century in which to build
# one. Every stock power plant in this repository raises steam and puts it
# through a turbine into a generator, and a turbine and a generator are both
# things 1890 cannot make -- steel frames, and a century and a half of
# metallurgy.
#
# So this plant imports the generators and nothing else.
#
#   river -> wheel -> belt -> pulley (geared up) -> generator -> 63 MW
#
# Everything to the left of the generator is local: pumps, wheels and a timber
# pulley, all of which a valley with a sawmill and a foundry can build. The
# generators came back through the fracture in crates, and their price is on the
# slice's machinery ledger rather than in this file -- four tiles of imported
# plant each, which is the cheapest electricity the first era can buy.
#
# What makes it work at all is the speed band. A generator will not make a
# megawatt below speed 4 and a river turns at speed 1, so the pulley here is not
# a convenience, it is the whole mechanism: geared up four times, two wheels'
# 120 rotary arrives as 85 at speed 4, against the 70 a generator draws.
#
# What makes it worth building is the comparison. Against `03-compact.machine`,
# which is what 2037 puts up without thinking about it:
#
#   compact steam plant   216 MW   two turbines, two generators   coal and water
#   this                  126 MW   two generators                 water
#
# Twenty-six tiles of imported machinery against eight, for the same order of
# power, because the first era already owns a prime mover. It does not need to
# import a way of making torque. It needs to import the one component that turns
# torque into electricity, and then it needs twice the ground and six times the
# water to use it.
#
# Two things about the scoreboard below, both of them honest rather than
# convenient. The plot and the water per megawatt are terrible, and that is the
# trade. And the brief's own guard rail -- "produce at least 100 MW from one fuel
# source" -- reports *zero* fuel sources, because there is no fire in this
# machine anywhere. That line was written in experiment 06 for a boiler. A plant
# that burns nothing is not cheating it; it is outside it.
#
# The failure mode is the era's own, too. Nothing here gets hot and nothing here
# burns. What it does is stop when the river is low, and come apart if anybody
# bolts something that shakes to the timber.

machine "Valley Hydro"
brief power

# ---- the first set
pump      P1  at 0,0    draws water
pump      P2  at 0,5    draws water
waterwheel W1 at 3,0
waterwheel W2 at 3,5
# Timber, and it stays timber: a generator shakes at 1, and this valley's
# difficulty is torque rather than vibration everywhere the crushers are not.
pulley    PL1 at 8,3    ratio -4
generator G1  at 11,3

# ---- the second, because one of these is 63 MW and the brief wants 100
pump      P3  at 0,10   draws water
pump      P4  at 0,15   draws water
waterwheel W3 at 3,10
waterwheel W4 at 3,15
pulley    PL2 at 8,13   ratio -4
generator G2  at 11,13

wire P1.water -> W1.water
wire P2.water -> W2.water
wire W1.rotary -> PL1.in  belt
wire W2.rotary -> PL1.in  belt
wire PL1.out -> G1.rotary

wire P3.water -> W3.water
wire P4.water -> W4.water
wire W3.rotary -> PL2.in  belt
wire W4.rotary -> PL2.in  belt
wire PL2.out -> G2.rotary
